The chart compares received and merged timestamps by week. A merge date is not an onset date or proof of a new incident. The export reaches 4 October, before the reported 5 October closure; it is not a live service view.

## Change the desk's next action

[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) replaces subject-only deduplication. The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) now uses that rule. The accepted decision 0001 remains unchanged as history; its 2024 review date is stale and does not establish current behavior. This review's date records when we checked the export, not when the desk or the product was fixed.

Match reports by verified cause and affected path. Equal subject lines can hide different problems; different subjects can describe the same cause. Missing tenant data or conflicting body evidence requires investigation rather than an automatic merge. Keep new hint reports out of the parser queue. Preserve received timestamps, report IDs and evidence when moving reports, so the count remains auditable.

<DedupeExplorer />

This explorer runs the supplied cause logic. It does not validate report bodies or prove a product fix. In particular, the one-day/timing reports above still need investigation.

## Closure and follow-up

The course accepted the two ticket allocations. The later desk update supports closing parser as completed and hint as not planned. Preserve those distinct reasons: a retired form is not a fixed hint. The five one-day/timing anomalies remain unresolved evidence in the historical allocation; closure alone does not explain them. Investigate any recurrence using refreshed reports, tenant data and body evidence rather than automatically merging into a closed ticket.

